; definite volume but no definite shape. takes the shape of its container.
; atoms/molecules move freely past each other — translational freedom.
; thermal energy > energy to escape lattice but < energy to leave surface.
; cohesive forces hold the liquid together — surface tension.
; 
; key properties:
; viscosity:       resistance to flow. honey > water > air.
; decreases with temperature (hot liquids flow faster).
; surface tension: cohesion at the surface. water striders, capillary action.
; vapour pressure: evaporation from surface. increases with temperature.
; incompressible:  liquids cannot be compressed significantly (unlike gases).
; 
; governed by Navier-Stokes equations (in the continuum limit).
; pressure transmits in all directions equally (Pascal's principle).
; density: liquids are dense — comparable to solids, ~1000x denser than gas.
; 
; water is the anomalous liquid: density maximum at 4°C (ice floats).
; the anomaly is because ice has open hexagonal hydrogen-bond structure.
; 
; in 3d / pratibimba: fluid simulation — SPH, grid-based Euler, or surface shader.
; for visual rendering: transparency + refraction (water, glass in liquid state).
; physics connections
; 3d / pratibimba

